.he "cu (1)                       9/30/26                       cu (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
cu
- call another system over a terminal line
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
cu device [ -t ] [ -s speed ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Cu
connects this machine to another one over the terminal line named
.bd
device
- /dev/ttyC, say - and passes characters between the two.
.sp
When the
.bd
cu
command is given, one process is set up to handle sending characters
to the remote system, one is set up to receive them.  Much of this is
not obvious to the user.
.sp
When
.bd
cu
is ready it says Connected.  Thereafter, whatever you type is
immediately sent to the remote port, and whatever is received from it
is sent to your terminal.  So, typically, you press a single key on
your terminal, Micronix takes it and re-transmits it to the remote
machine, which will usually send it back, at which point Micronix
receives it and sends it to your terminal - and you see the character
on your screen.
.sp
There is definitely an upper limit to the baud rate you can use for
the remote port; 1200 baud is the highest recommended.
.sp
A line that begins with ~ is a command to
.bd
cu
rather than a line to the other machine:
.sp
.in +10
.ti -10
~.	Leave cu and hang up the line.  ~^D does the same.

.ti -10
~~	Send a line that really does begin with a ~.

.ti -10
~<file	Send the named file over the line.

.ti -10
~!	Run a login shell here, with your terminal as it usually
is.  ~!cmd runs just that command, with its output left on your
terminal.

.ti -10
~$cmd	Run cmd here with its output sent over the line instead
of to your terminal.

.ti -10
~%put from [to]	Send a local file to the remote machine.

.ti -10
~%take from [to]	Fetch a file from the remote machine.
.in -10
.sp
The two last are builtins: what they do is push a short shell
procedure at the remote machine, so they want a remote that runs a
shell of the same shape.  Note that only text files may be copied
reliably.  This need not be a drawback, however, because any file can
be converted into a text file, transferred, and then converted back
again.
.sp
Both the sending and the receiving processes recognize
.sp
.nf
	~>file

	~>
.fi
as starting and ending delimiters for file diversion, so that in
order to copy a file from a foreign system to the local one, all you
have to do is get the foreign system to generate:
.sp
.nf
	~>filename
	Text of file ...
	more file ...
	~>
.fi
That is, a beginning marker, the text of the file, and then an ending
marker.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-s speed	Set the speed of the line.  The speeds are 110, 134,
150, 300 and 1200; 300 is the default.

.ti -10
-t	Do not split the conversation in two.  There is no local
terminal to read and write here - the line is the terminal - so cu
reads and writes it by itself and asks the driver for no echo and no
raw mode.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLE
.in +5
.br
To set up communication over a modem, get your hardware set up and
then type:
.sp
.in +5
.ti +5
.br
cu /dev/ttyC -s 300
.in -5
.sp
In this example the modem would be plugged into Micronix port C (the
third one).
.sp
Typically, one must reverse the transmit and receive lines in the
cable running from the computer to the modem.  One reverses the wires
running to pins 2 and 3 of the connector at one end or the other.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
Cu opens the line it is given and nothing else.  v7's cu is built
around an automatic calling unit: it takes a telephone number, opens a
dialer and dials, and the line to use and the dialer to use are its
own options.  Micronix has no such device, and cu (1) here has always
named the line as its argument, so the -l and -a options and the
dialing are not in this command.  A line the driver wants carrier on
is waited for when it is opened; to drop the call, leave cu.
.sp
~! and ~$ are v7's and are not on the 1982 page: they run a shell
here, which is a way of getting at this machine's files without
leaving the connection.
.sp
The 1982 page says cu prints a "~" when it is ready.  This one says
Connected, as v7's does.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
stty (1), login (1), write (1)
